[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Which surface should the preferences storage serve? → Browser webapp (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Browser it is — [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, per-device.

  [1mNext fork: how much of the feature is in scope.[0m This matters now because it decides whether [38;5;153mindex.html[39m changes at all,
  and whether we need a preference that visibly [3mdoes[0m something (which is the only way to prove persistence actually
  works end to end).

  - [1mStorage layer only[0m — a [38;5;153mpreferences.js[39m module with get/set/remove/clear over [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, plus a schema and
    defaults. Nothing in the page changes; nothing reads it yet. Fastest to land and easy to test in isolation, but
    "settings persist across sessions" stays unverified in the real app, and the first consumer may reveal the API is
    wrong.
  - [1mStorage + one wired preference[0m — the module, plus one real preference driven from the existing login form (e.g.
    "remember username", which repopulates the field on reload). Proves the round-trip in the actual page with a small
    surface. Costs a modest edit to [38;5;153mindex.html[39m and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m.
  - [1mStorage + a settings UI[0m — the module, plus a settings panel with several preferences (theme, remember-me, etc.) and
    the markup/styling to host it. Most complete, but it's a UI project bolted onto a login stub, and most of the work
    becomes layout rather than persistence.

  I recommend [1mstorage + one wired preference[0m. It's the smallest scope that actually demonstrates the thing you asked for
  — settings surviving a session — and it forces the module's API to be validated by a real caller instead of only by
  tests. The settings UI is cheap to add afterward [3mprovided[0m the store isn't built around one specific preference, which
  the design will guard against.
